# 18. CSRF REVISION

## Definition

> CSRF tricks a victim's browser into making an unwanted request to a website where the victim is authenticated.

### Important condition

Usually:

```text
Authenticated browser
+
State-changing action
+
Insufficient CSRF protection
```

---

# 19. CSRF DEFENSE

```text
CSRF Token
+
SameSite Cookie Policy
+
Origin Validation
+
Appropriate Referer Validation
+
Safe HTTP Methods
```

### Remember

> **Authentication does NOT equal CSRF protection.**

---

# 20. CSRF VS XSS

### XSS

Attacker-controlled content executes in the browser.

### CSRF

Victim browser is tricked into sending an unwanted request.

```text
XSS → EXECUTE
CSRF → REQUEST
```

---

# 21. CORS VS CSRF

### CORS

Controls whether browser JavaScript can access cross-origin responses according to server policy.

### CSRF

Concerns unwanted state-changing requests.

```text
CORS → Response access
CSRF → Request forgery
```

They are not interchangeable defenses.

---

# 22. COOKIE SECURITY REVISION

Important attributes:

| Attribute | Main Purpose |
|---|---|
| Secure | Send cookie only over HTTPS |
| HttpOnly | Prevent JavaScript from directly reading cookie |
| SameSite | Control cross-site cookie behavior |

### Memory

> **H-S-S**

HttpOnly → Secure → SameSite

